WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:13.200
If you’ve watched any number of nature documentaries,
you’ve probably seen some variation on this scene.

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:18.610
In place of ciliates, you might see a lion
stalking its prey, or a shark lunging for

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:21.550
its next meal.

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:28.590
The tension builds, the motion of spring loaded
muscles matched by creeping music as the predator

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:31.550
waits for its moment to strike.

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:35.440
Sometimes the outcome is similar to this feeding
frenzy.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:41.720
But other times, the kindly voice of an elderly
narrator assures you that the prey has escaped.

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:45.720
All is well...this time.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:51.629
The microcosmos is filled with its own versions
of these tales of predation and survival.

00:00:51.629 --> 00:00:59.780
This isn’t a world built on nocturnal predators
or camouflage that shields predator and prey alike.

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:07.980
These are hunters who have to resort to other
methods to find their food, like this lacrymaria’s

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:10.780
sweeping approach to the hunt.

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:16.380
While this protist may look delicate, it is
an incredibly successful hunter.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:23.280
It can extend the tip of its neck out to seven
times the length of its body, and then retract

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:30.000
it back in a matter of seconds, and it uses
this motion to seek out its prey.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.460
Each time the lacrymaria sticks its head out, what it’s really doing is gambling.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:41.979
It’s playing the odds that as it whips around,
it’ll make contact with food, and it can

00:01:41.979 --> 00:01:48.360
play this game for minutes and minutes, using
the dense cilia on its head to search until,

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:55.700
like this lucky lacrymaria, an unfortunate
ciliate comes into contact and quickly becomes prey.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:05.260
But not all threats in the microcosmos move
so quickly.

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:09.800
Sometimes, it's the stationary organisms you
have to look out for.

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:17.180
Collothecas, for example, are a type of rotifer
that are sessile, meaning they do not move around.

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:24.610
Instead, it attaches its foot to a surface
and just waits, letting its exceptionally

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:30.450
long cilia hang out like cat's whiskers to
draw in food.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:35.260
And from this angle, the rotifer seems to
make catching food look easy, like it just

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:40.099
needs to sit around and wait for a flagellate
to stroll into its mouth.

00:02:40.099 --> 00:02:44.190
From the flagellate’s perspective, the situation
is a little more dire.

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:52.819
It was just casually living its life one second, and then trapped in the belly of a rotifer the next.

00:02:52.819 --> 00:02:57.739
Take a wrong turn in the microcosmos, and
you might find yourself facing other threats

00:02:57.739 --> 00:03:02.080
as well, not just at the hands of a predator.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:08.269
This ciliate came from a lake that was nearly
frozen, and as we prepared it for the microscope,

00:03:08.269 --> 00:03:13.680
the organism had to contend with a very rapid
change in temperature.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:20.680
Unable to withstand that change in its surroundings,
the ciliate died, its membrane rupturing and

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:22.459
emptying out.

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:26.940
All of that order, immediately returned to
disorder.

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:41.590
This other ciliate was able to survive the
initial temperature shock that took its neighbor.

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:48.440
But as it nears the spilled contents of the
dying ciliate, the healthy one suddenly becomes

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:50.460
much less healthy.

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:53.159
And it too bursts.

00:03:53.159 --> 00:03:58.549
We've seen this before on our channel, and
it still remains a mystery no matter how many

00:03:58.549 --> 00:04:05.600
times we watch an organism experience this
strange, contagious death.

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:10.689
Perhaps there was a change in osmotic pressure
around the first dying ciliate, which would

00:04:10.689 --> 00:04:14.600
have then damaged the membrane of the healthy
one wandering by.

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:19.000
But some threats can be anticipated.

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:26.080
This homalozoon is a single-celled hunter,
and it is on the prowl, searching for something

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:31.160
delicious to eat in a veritable buffet of
paramecium.

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:37.090
And when one potential prey comes near, the
homalozoon winds up, using structures on its

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:44.360
ectoplasm called extrusomes that act like
harpoons to pierce their target and inject

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:45.780
toxins into them.

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:51.220
But where you might expect a victorious hunter,
this homalozoon recoils back.

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:56.330
It all happens so quickly, it’s hard for
us to see exactly what’s going on.

00:04:56.330 --> 00:05:04.139
It turns out that the homalozoon was not the
only organism coming into battle armed.

00:05:04.139 --> 00:05:11.039
The paramecium had its own defenses too, a
4 micron long structure called a trichocyst

00:05:11.039 --> 00:05:17.120
that can extend to a longer length in a matter
of milliseconds in response to some kind of

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:21.240
stimulus---like another organism trying to
injure it.

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:25.210
The homalozoon is able to get its attack in,
stunning the paramecium.

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:32.260
But it is also left confused by the paramecium’s
response, as it begins nibbling away on those

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:36.940
protective trichocysts instead of the delicious
microbe itself.

00:05:36.940 --> 00:05:44.910
And in the meantime, the paramecium is able
to recover and escape… this time.

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:50.440
But sometimes, these natural protections can
fail you.

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:56.039
Testate amoebas form protection out of bits
of sand from their environment, sticking them

00:05:56.039 --> 00:05:59.699
together with an adhesive to form a shell.

00:05:59.699 --> 00:06:04.930
It’s difficult for us to get a good image
of this shell, but it’s that dark shape

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:06.289
moving through the frame.

00:06:06.289 --> 00:06:11.600
And like any shell, it’s great protection--as
long as you can stay inside it.

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:18.819
But the amoeba has things it has to do, and
that means it needs to extend its body outwards

00:06:18.819 --> 00:06:21.110
using its pseudopodia.

00:06:21.110 --> 00:06:28.350
And in that moment of vulnerability, the coleps
strikes.

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:33.350
Coleps may be tiny, but they have extrusomes
that pack quite a punch.

00:06:33.350 --> 00:06:40.460
The toxins they inject into the amoeba do
their work quickly, tearing the pseudopodium apart.

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:45.820
And like a pack of scavengers following the
smell of blood in water, these coleps can

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:48.410
sense the dying amoeba.

00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:56.370
They swarm around and consume the bits and
pieces, the undigested amoeba visible inside

00:06:56.370 --> 00:07:02.099
their bodies while the shell sits uselessly
next to them.

00:07:02.099 --> 00:07:06.050
But the microcosmos is remarkable in its resiliency.

00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:12.060
And so let us end with this ciliate, which
has dealt with some kind of damage.

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:16.889
There are a number of microbes that are able
to repair themselves, some of which we’ve

00:07:16.889 --> 00:07:18.300
talked about on this channel.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:21.690
But we haven’t been able to show you one
in action.

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:27.040
We don’t know what caused this ciliate’s
membrane to rupture in this way, where we

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:31.820
can see these bits and pieces almost poking
out of it.

00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:38.370
But as it turns and turns and waves its cilia,
we can see its membrane beginning to knit

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:44.560
back together until finally at the end, it
almost seems as if there was never any damage

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:46.190
to begin with.

00:07:46.190 --> 00:07:53.420
The ciliate has survived this day, and all
is well, no matter what may happen tomorrow.

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:04.780
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:09.160
And if you like what we do, you should be thanking these people on the screen right now.

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:12.060
They are our patrons on Patreon.

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:15.280
I know there are some of you right now, looking at your own name.

00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:16.360
So, thank you.

